---
// The navigation across the Nightingale Nightmare pages, and it now spans two levels.
//
// **A race is the recurring thing; an event is one running of it in one year**, and the paths
// say so — `/nn/` and `/nn/course/` are about the race, `/nn/2026/` and everything beneath it
// is about one running. A navigation that mixed the two would offer somebody standing on the
// evergreen course page a link to a particular year's race day, which is a link to a fact the
// page they are on has deliberately declined to hold.
//
// So there are two rows:
//
//   the race       Race · Course             on every Nightingale Nightmare page
//   the running    2026 · Race day · Spectators   on year pages only
//
// **The running row is derived from the path, not from the database.** A page at
// `/nn/2026/race-day/` knows it is 2026 because that is its own address, so the nav needs no
// request-time data, no rewriting and no round trip — which is what lets it be right on the
// content pages, which the Worker does not touch at all.
//
// ## Why an evergreen page shows only the race row
//
// Because it does not know which running is current and must not guess. `/nn/` finds out —
// the Worker asks `entries.current_entry_state('nn')` and paints the links into the page
// body — but `/nn/course/` and `/nn/privacy/` are plain static pages, and giving them a
// year would mean either a database call on every content page view or a year written into
// markup. The first is a new failure mode on pages that currently have none; the second is
// the thing this whole slice exists to remove.
//
// The cost is one extra tap: from the course page, "Race" and then this year's race. That is
// the trade, it is small, and it is worth less than a year hard-coded in a component.
//
// **Still no JavaScript, no dropdown and no hamburger.** Five links at most, and a menu that
// has to be opened is more machinery than the thing it hides. Two rows wrap to more lines on
// a narrow screen and that is all they do.
//
// ## Why the current page is derived rather than passed in
//
// A `current` prop would be a second place to state which page this is, and the failure it
// invites is silent: a page that passes the wrong one renders a nav that lies about where
// you are, and nothing catches it but a person noticing. `Astro.url.pathname` is already
// the answer, so it is the one used. The trailing slash is normalised because
// `trailingSlash: 'always'` makes `/nn/course/` the only real address and a mismatch here
// would simply drop `aria-current` with no other symptom.
//
// ## `aria-current="page"`, and why the styling does not stand in for it
//
// The yellow and the underline say "you are here" to somebody looking at it. `aria-current`
// says it to somebody who is not, and it is the attribute — not the colour — that assistive
// technology reads. Both are present because neither covers the other's reader.

const raceLinks = [
  { href: '/nn/', label: 'Race' },
  { href: '/nn/course/', label: 'Course' },
] as const;

const path = Astro.url.pathname.endsWith('/')
  ? Astro.url.pathname
  : `${Astro.url.pathname}/`;

// Four digits directly beneath `/nn/`, and nothing else. `/nn/course/` is not a year, and a
// page that is not under a running gets no running row rather than a guessed one.
const year = /^\/nn\/(\d{4})\//.exec(path)?.[1] ?? null;

const runningLinks =
  year === null
    ? []
    : [
        { href: `/nn/${year}/`, label: year },
        { href: `/nn/${year}/race-day/`, label: 'Race day' },
        { href: `/nn/${year}/spectators/`, label: 'Spectators' },
      ];
---

{
  /* One `<nav>` with one accessible name, and two lists inside it. Two `<nav>` elements would
  be two landmarks a screen-reader user has to tell apart, for one navigation that happens to
  be drawn on two rows — and naming them would mean inventing words ("race navigation",
  "2026 navigation") for a distinction the links themselves already make. */
}
<nav class="nn-nav" aria-label="Nightingale Nightmare">
  <ul>
    {
      raceLinks.map(({ href, label }) => (
        <li>
          <a href={href} aria-current={href === path ? 'page' : undefined}>
            {label}
          </a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  {
    runningLinks.length > 0 && (
      <ul class="nn-nav-running">
        {runningLinks.map(({ href, label }) => (
          <li>
            <a href={href} aria-current={href === path ? 'page' : undefined}>
              {label}
            </a>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ul>
    )
  }
</nav>
